Non-Restorative Sleep Syndrome
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Subjectively unrefreshing sleep despite >7 hours duration | Daytime somnolence or cognitive fatigue | Normal polysomnography (No OSA/PLMD)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-010
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of daytime somnolence and severe morning headaches requiring urgent assessment to exclude severe sleep apnea or carbon dioxide narcosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required due to profound morning grogginess and severe headaches. Immediate clinical evaluation was mandated to exclude acute hypercapnia secondary to undiagnosed severe obstructive sleep apnea, preventing respiratory compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: No matter if I sleep for 10 hours, I wake up feeling like I've been hit by a truck and my head is pounding.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a persistent feeling of unrefreshing sleep despite adequate duration. There is profound morning grogginess and sleep inertia, leading to excessive daytime sleepiness, though lacking discrete narcoleptic sleep attacks. The patient notes generalized mild cognitive slowing or brain fog and intermittent tension-type headaches upon waking. There is subjective physical exhaustion throughout the day and frequent brief awakenings remembered vaguely. The patient admits to difficulty sustaining attention during monotonous tasks and mild mood irritability.
Functional Status: Independent, but struggling to maintain occupational duties due to exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of motor vehicle accident due to daytime sleepiness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 mmHg | HR: 72 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted with notable periorbital darkening, but alert and oriented.
Physical Exam: Airway assessment reveals a Mallampati Score of II with no obvious retrognathia or tonsillar hypertrophy. Cardiopulmonary examination is normal. Systemic examination incidentally notes a history of Raynaud's phenomenon (biphasic color changes in digits on exposure to cold) and mild polyarthralgia in the small joints of the hands. Previous rheumatology records indicate a positive ANA. Neurological exam reveals no focal deficits.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom features heavy blackout curtains. Multiple electronic devices are active near the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No sedatives or hypnotics currently prescribed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-Restorative Sleep Syndrome - Status: Stable, exacerbated by poor sleep hygiene and underlying rheumatological fatigue.
Differentials:

Severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out clinically for acute risk due to the absence of witnessed prolonged apneas, normal SpO2 on room air, and lack of obvious anatomical airway obstruction.

Carbon Dioxide Narcosis - Ruled out due to alert mental status, normal respiratory rate, and absence of bounding pulses or asterixis.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out as primary acute cause due to the specific physiological nature of the unrefreshing sleep architecture, though mood is secondarily impacted.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Driving risk).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This urgent evaluation was necessary to assess severe morning headaches and extreme unrefreshing sleep, which raised the immediate suspicion for nocturnal hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the risk of severe undiagnosed obstructive sleep apnea precipitating respiratory failure. Thorough physical examination confirmed a patent airway, normal oxygen saturation, and intact neurological status, firmly excluding acute hypercapnia. By conducting this targeted assessment, the immediate risk of respiratory arrest was eliminated, safely averting an emergency department admission and allowing for coordinated outpatient polysomnography scheduling.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Sleep hygiene overhaul initiated. The expected investigation results include polysomnography demonstrating normal sleep architecture without severe obstructive sleep apnea or significant periodic limb movements, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To improve sleep architecture, physically remove all electronic devices from the bedroom one hour before your intended sleep time; eliminating blue light exposure will reinforce natural melatonin production and deepen your sleep cycles.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Explicit clinical instruction provided to strictly avoid operating a motor vehicle or heavy machinery until formally reviewed by the GP due to the severe daytime somnolence.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for a formal sleep study referral and rheumatology review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient is observed to stop breathing during sleep for prolonged periods, wakes up gasping for air and clutching their chest, or becomes profoundly confused upon waking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal polysomnography (sleep study); sleep disorders are multifactorial.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course is chronic and fluctuating, requiring a highly structured, multi-disciplinary approach to sleep hygiene, cognitive behavioral therapy, and underlying disease management.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your morning headache becomes suddenly explosive, or if you develop new visual changes, weakness, or severe shortness of breath.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sleeping pills are strictly contraindicated tonight because they alter natural sleep architecture and can dangerously suppress your breathing if you have an undiagnosed airway obstruction.
Actionable Steps: Establish a strict, non-negotiable wake-up time every single day regardless of how tired you feel to reset your circadian rhythm.

